  "surprise": "683 at 8 loops (seeds 676/699/673) against 690 (687/715/669): -7 (-11/-16/+4), inside noise, discard by the rule. metric and metric_diag are within noise of each other (683 against 678 at 2 loops: 664/710/661), so this model is no better at 8 loops than at 2. The speed half of the prediction held in full. Pretraining took 4,777/4,896/4,700 steps (1.40x 502; predicted 4,900-5,400, just under) and val loss at 2 loops was 0.0808/0.0784/0.0848 (mean 0.0813 against 0.0866). The fine-tune cost 74-104 s a round against 120-163 s, so 10/9/9 RL rounds fit against 7/7/6, RL solved 1,221 targets (1200/1253/1211) against 1,203, and last-round 1-loop accuracy on RL targets was 0.781/0.806/0.781 against 0.730/0.740/0.731 (0.750/0.778/0.763 already at round 7). The untrained depth did not break: val loss at 8 loops was within 0.0002 of 4 loops on every seed, and on the pretrained model the round-1 retries cracked resistant targets 15.8% of the time at 2 loops, 19.0% at 4 and 19.9% at 8 (502, trained on 1-8: 14.6/19.3/20.7), so a model that never trained past 4 loops gets the same lift from 8 as one that did. Pooled over all rounds 8 loops stayed within 0.4 points of 4 as predicted (9.4/10.9/11.3% at 2/4/8), and per-sample dev accuracy at 8 loops rose to 0.484/0.487/0.480 from 0.466/0.489/0.460. What went the other way is the gap this loop exists to grow. metric minus diag fell to +4 (12/-11/12) from +18 (23/19/13); on seed 1, 8 loops solved 11 fewer theorems than 2 loops (the pre-registered readout falsifier needed two such seeds, so it did not fire). Per-sample dev accuracy at 2 loops rose by 0.031 (0.470/0.486/0.470 against 0.435/0.461/0.436) and at 8 loops by 0.011, so 8 loops now add +0.008 per sample (0.014/0.001/0.010) where 502 had +0.028. In RL the edge of 8 loops over 4 on resistant targets that 502 showed (15.9% against 13.8% pooled, 14.9 against 12.2 in rounds 3-6) is gone (11.3 against 10.9; 8.2 against 8.1). All retry rates were below my predicted 11-16%: at matched rounds 3-6 resistant targets were cracked 6.4/8.1/8.2% of the time at 2/4/8 loops against 502's 11.1/12.2/14.9%, with failed sets of about the same size on seed 0 (560/461/407/367 against 540/450/390/343). The surprise is that higher per-sample accuracy at both depths bought no theorems. The model got sharper, not broader: at 8 loops, dev theorems solved on 48 or more of the 64 attempts rose to 497 (501/496/494) from 483 (469/503/476), theorems solved on only 1-15 attempts fell to 89 (85/95/86) from 103 (106/111/93), and unsolved went from 418 to 425. The 2-3 extra RL rounds added 44/42/33 targets after round 7's first pass. Interpretation, not established: the metric counts the tail of rarely-solved theorems, which is fed by the spread of the sampling distribution; more fine-tune rounds and better-trained shallow passes concentrate probability on proofs the model already finds, which raises per-sample accuracy and lowers the crack rate on what is left. That is the third time more rounds or lower val loss failed to reach the metric (504: 9/9/8 rounds; 505: val 0.079). On depth: the 8-loop state of a 1-4 model is a usable fixed point, as the spike said, but the small advantage of 8 loops over 2 after RL (most of 502's +18) depends on training at 5-8 loops, or on 2 loops being under-trained in the 1-8 model, which this run cannot separate; either way the 1-8 range is not pure cost for this metric. No crash; totals 3,335/3,439/3,435 s. Next: the cheaper 1-4 model is equal within noise, so the saved time is better spent on spread than on rounds (more attempts or a higher oracle temperature on the retries, fewer fine-tune steps per round); and a 1-4 range with an occasional 8-loop batch would separate the two readings of the lost gap.",
